{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-95054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={t}^{-2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{2}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{-2}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{-2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{e}^{t}}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{-2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-2({t}^{2})({t}^{-3})+2{e}^{-t}({e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-95054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}"] ["math" "\\frac{2(t{e}^{t}-1)}{{e}^{t}}"] ["math" "\\frac{3({t}^{2}+1)}{t}"] ["math" "\\frac{{t}^{2}{e}^{t}+2}{{e}^{t}}"] ["math" "t(t-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=2x-x{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2-{y}^{2})\\,i-2xy\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=3{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-37099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2+9{t}^{3}+6{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{t}-3t-3t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{t}-3{t}^{2}+36{t}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{2}-12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y={e}^{-t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-36211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-2t}"] ["math" "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}"] ["math" "2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}"] ["math" "4{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-95059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)=\\pi t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\pi t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j)\\cdot (\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\pi {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-95052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t"] ["math" "\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t"] ["math" "\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t"] ["math" "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)"] ["math" "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,k \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-95070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t{cos}^{3}⁡2t"] ["math" "{cos}^{2}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y,z)=x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+(t+1)\\,j+(t-1)\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,i+2xy\\,j-2xz\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $y=t+1$ and $z=t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =[(t+1)^{2}-(t-1)^{2}]i+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot (t+1)\\,j-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot (t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =4t\\,i+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4t\\,i+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot 1-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & =4+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-95142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1)"] ["math" "4(t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)"] ["math" "4+4t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "4t(t-1)"] ["math" "4t(t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t-1}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{xyz}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{xyz}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{xyz}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j+\\frac{1}{z}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y={e}^{t-1},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t-1}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t-1}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t-1}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,k)\\cdot (i+{e}^{t-1}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t-1}}{{e}^{t-1}}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t+1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-95072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{t-1}(1-t)"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{2t+1}{t}"] ["math" "\\frac{2t{e}^{t}+1}{t}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{t}+1}{t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2t+1}{t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y,z)=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,k \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y={e}^{t},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-95146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})"] ["math" "{e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})"] ["math" "{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)"] ["math" "{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+{e}^{y}\\,j+\\frac{1}{z}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,y={t}^{2},$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\,j+t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\,j+t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-95149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{e}^{{t}^{2}}-1"] ["math" "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t+{e}^{{t}^{2}}+t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+\\frac{{e}^{{t}^{2}}}{{t}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-95149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=0$ given that $∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}+1)\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}[(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =2{e}^{2(0)}((2(0)+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))+2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-37109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-37109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{ }j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-167498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2e"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{e}"] ["math" "e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-167497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2\\pi "] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+t\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\frac{t}{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-94872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2-\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$ and $r(t)=2\\sqrt{t}\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=-2\\sqrt{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-(-2\\sqrt{t})\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\sqrt{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})i) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))] & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-94878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-167499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{9}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-167499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,4,\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},3{y}^{2},2xz⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=4$ and $z=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3})^{2},3(4)^{2},2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-3)\\cdot 1+48\\cdot 0+2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3+0+2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =3(2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =12-3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-94888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-7"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-36227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}-1"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-36227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-1,3,2t⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t-1,$ $y=3$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(3)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(2t)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 1+\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 0+\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2+8t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =\\frac{10(0)-2}{5(0)^{2}-2(0)+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-94891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{12}{17}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{13}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t-1⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=2t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2{t}^{2},-2(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,2⟩ \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t+(2-4t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-8t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =4(0)^{3}-8(0)+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a854bbb4a6ea55510a8bf6902d68f76ecdcf00e2c1d67806ad6c764f7f6c8a7e"} {"question" "q-94884" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94884" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "q-94884" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/state.json" "file_sha256" "18610f27c99997c8bae2ea9eaa384710190cc2575a7119299a19b2803e143c94"} {"question" "e-3694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/example-3694.json" "file_sha256" "6b94888703288b456cd3e8fb11c70e6499b1a8d4ff46a30d5a5d7a89cb4a731c"} {"question" "e-3694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/example-3694.json" "file_sha256" "6b94888703288b456cd3e8fb11c70e6499b1a8d4ff46a30d5a5d7a89cb4a731c"} {"question" "e-3695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/example-3695.json" "file_sha256" "6bce6f56a20077bbe2a29e23d56e64f041e48df8026046d58142ce17ff4fcb38"} {"question" "e-3695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{xz}{y}\\,j+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/example-3695.json" "file_sha256" "6bce6f56a20077bbe2a29e23d56e64f041e48df8026046d58142ce17ff4fcb38"} {"question" "e-8282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/example-8282.json" "file_sha256" "59cea8d3aa67c53aa3e3e3cc7e7473b55a3e114fa98da861a3ebb9801a13bbf8"} {"question" "e-8282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/example-8282.json" "file_sha256" "59cea8d3aa67c53aa3e3e3cc7e7473b55a3e114fa98da861a3ebb9801a13bbf8"} {"question" "e-3696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/example-3696.json" "file_sha256" "b45fdbde9cb5c46d0195e7433f774f8d698c945d07129762399ccd07cd944089"} {"question" "e-3696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069490/example-3696.json" "file_sha256" "b45fdbde9cb5c46d0195e7433f774f8d698c945d07129762399ccd07cd944089"}] "usage" {"e-3696" [] "e-3694" [] "e-3695" [] "q-94872" [] "e-8282" [] "q-36211" []} "basis" 2376 "retractions" [[17592186124871 :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2}$ and $\\mathbf{r} ( t ) = {e}^{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} + {e}^{- t} \\, \\mathbf{j} ,$, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} t} [ f ( \\mathbf{r} ( t ) ) ] .$."] [17592186124871 :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=\\langle2x,2y\\rangle$, so along the curve it is $\\langle2e^t,2e^{-t}\\rangle$. Since $\\mathbf r'=\\langle e^t,-e^{-t}\\rangle$, the vector chain rule gives $$\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)=2e^{2t}-2e^{-2t}.$$"] [17592186125162 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} t} [ f ( \\mathbf{r} ( t ) ) ]$ at $t = \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) = \\sin   y \\, \\mathbf{i} + x \\cos   y \\, \\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r} ( t ) = \\frac{t}{2} \\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\, \\mathbf{j} .$."] [17592186125162 :question/worked-solution "At $t=\\pi/2$, the point on the curve is $(\\pi/4,\\pi/2)$. Thus $\\nabla f=\\langle1,0\\rangle$, while $\\mathbf r'=\\langle1/2,1\\rangle$. Their dot product gives the required derivative: $1\\cdot\\tfrac12+0\\cdot1=\\tfrac12$."] [17592186124191 :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j$, compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))]$."] [17592186124191 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(x^{2} + 2y^{3})i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(x^{2} + 2y^{3})j \\\\\n&= 2xi + 6y^{2}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(r(t)) &= 2(\\sin t)i + 6(e^{t})^{2}j \\\\\n&= 2\\sin ti + 6e^{2t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{\\prime }(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t})j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti + e^{t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= \\nabla f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{\\prime }(t) \\\\\n&= (2\\sin ti + 6e^{2t}j) \\cdot (\\cos ti + e^{t}j) \\\\\n&= 2\\sin t\\cos t + 6e^{2t} \\cdot e^{t} \\\\\n&= \\sin 2t + 6e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124192 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t \\, \\mathbf j +t^3 \\, \\mathbf k$, compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))]$."] [17592186124192 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(xz\\ln y)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(xz\\ln y)j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(xz\\ln y)k \\\\\n&= z\\ln yi + \\frac{xz}{y}j + x\\ln yk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2$, $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(r(t)) &= t^{3}\\ln ti + t^{4}j + t^{2}\\ln tk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{\\prime }(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3})k \\\\\n&= 2ti + j + 3t^{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= \\nabla f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{\\prime }(t) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}\\ln ti + t^{4}j + t^{2}\\ln tk) \\cdot (2ti + j + 3t^{2}k) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}\\ln t) \\cdot 2t + (t^{4}) \\cdot 1 + (t^{2}\\ln t) \\cdot 3t^{2} \\\\\n&= 2t^{4}\\ln t + t^{4} + 3t^{4}\\ln t \\\\\n&= t^{4}(5\\ln t + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124322 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j$."] [17592186124322 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives $\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)$. At $t=\\pi$, $\\mathbf r(\\pi)=(0,-1)$, so $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(\\pi))=\\langle-3,-1\\rangle$. Also $\\mathbf r'(\\pi)=\\langle\\cos\\pi,-\\sin\\pi\\rangle=\\langle-1,0\\rangle$. Therefore the required rate is $(-3)(-1)+(-1)(0)=3$."] [17592186124193 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4$."] [17592186124193 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)$. Here $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\langle2x,-2y,-1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'=\\langle\\cos t,-\\sin t,1\\rangle$. Thus $$\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\langle2\\sin t,-2\\cos t,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle\\cos t,-\\sin t,1\\rangle=4\\sin t\\cos t-1.$$ Evaluating at $t=\\pi/4$ gives $4(\\sqrt2/2)^2-1=1$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122249 17592186122250 17592186122251 17592186122252 17592186122253 17592186123995 17592186123996 17592186123997 17592186123998 17592186123999] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122248 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122248 :answer-field/id] [17592186122248 :answer-field/key] [17592186122248 :answer-field/type] [17592186123994 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123994 :answer-field/id] [17592186123994 :answer-field/key] [17592186123994 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
